  "worked_solution": "We can evaluate this limit in terms of the constant $c$ using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-c}{3\\cdot {2}^{x}} & =\\frac{{4}^{0}-c}{3\\cdot {2}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-c}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $2,$ we can equate the above with $2,$ and then solve for $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-c}{3} & =2 \\\\ 1-c & =6 \\\\ -c & =6-1 \\\\ c & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
